\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{Grassmann variables and finite fermion integrals}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Can electrons, which never share a state, be described in the
probability-based setting? The [spatial Dirac operator](fdip-000R)
has a real matrix in its chosen basis and is
[skew-adjoint](fdip-000S), so its real restriction satisfies
#{D^T=-D}. For commuting real coordinates #{x}, transposition gives
#{x^TDx=-x^TDx=0}. The weight #{e^{-x^TDx/2}} is therefore constant,
and adding a scalar mass leaves only #{m x^Tx} in
#{x^T(D+mI)x}: this quadratic expression loses the spatial operator.}

\p{Electrons are \newvocab{fermions}: their multiparticle amplitudes
change sign when two identical particles are exchanged
\citet{v2, Section 9.1, pp. 121-122}{etingof2024mathematical}. Exclusion
concerns the same complete one-particle state, including spin, not
merely the same spatial orbital. The [exterior algebra](ca-000E)
encodes the alternating sign through generators satisfying
#{\psi_i\psi_j=-\psi_j\psi_i} and #{\psi_i^2=0} over
#{\mathbb R} or #{\mathbb C}. These are \newvocab{Grassmann variables}.
Their use here is an algebraic model of that sign rule, not a derivation
of electron physics or a claim that measured fermion outcomes lack
ordinary probabilities.}

\p{Integrating commuting Gaussian fields produces inverse powers
of a determinant. For Grassmann variables, [ordered top-coefficient
extraction](fdip-008F) instead gives a [determinant](fdip-008H) for
independent paired variables and a [Pfaffian](fdip-008J) for an
alternating quadratic form. Two generators fix the signs before the
general identities; the normalized [Wick rule](fdip-008K) additionally
requires invertibility. This algebraic integration is not a positive
measure. Its relation to the earlier [Clifford algebra](fdip-001B) is
a vector-space comparison. Elimination, covariant lattice Dirac
operators and their doubling problem are the later questions.}

